<h2 class="ds-section-title">Why agents stall</h2>

<p class="consulting-lede">
  In our experience, the model is the last thing to blame. These are the three reasons an agent that demos well stops
  paying for itself in a real repo.
</p>

<div class="consulting-faults">
  <div class="consulting-fault">
    <span class="consulting-fault-label">No feedback loop</span>
    <p>
      The agent cannot tell whether it is right, so you become the compiler. If the suite takes fourteen minutes and
      five tests fail on main, a better model changes nothing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="consulting-fault">
    <span class="consulting-fault-label">No context</span>
    <p>
      No rules, no conventions, no map of the code. Every session begins by rediscovering the codebase, at your expense,
      and the agent writes the same wrong thing twice.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="consulting-fault">
    <span class="consulting-fault-label">No measurement</span>
    <p>
      Adoption is a feeling. Without a before and an after, nobody can tell whether the spend bought anything, and the
      renewal gets decided on vibes.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="ds-section-title">What we do</h2>

<p class="consulting-lede">
  Three engagements, in the order most teams need them. Take one and stop, or run all three. If the constraint turns out
  to be somewhere else in the stack, keep reading.
</p>

<div class="consulting-offers">
  <article class="consulting-offer">
    <span class="consulting-offer-label">01 / Audit</span>
    <h3 class="consulting-offer-title">Find where the agent loses time</h3>
    <p>
      We read the repo, run your agents on real tasks, and measure where the time and the tokens go: the rules it loads,
      the tools it calls, the checks it waits for, the review it creates. You get the five changes worth making, in
      order, with the evidence behind each one.
    </p>
    <p class="consulting-offer-proof">
      We ran the same kind of measurement before we trusted sub-agents: four of them finished
      <a href="/writing/how-coding-agents-work/sub-agents/">11 seconds faster than one and read 73% more tokens</a>. An
      audit gives you that answer for your repo.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="consulting-offer">
    <span class="consulting-offer-label">02 / Setup</span>
    <h3 class="consulting-offer-title">Install the configuration</h3>
    <p>
      Rules and context files, skills, tool and permission boundaries, and the verification loop that lets the agent
      check its own work. We open a branch you can review, and we leave the reasoning in the files themselves.
    </p>
    <p class="consulting-offer-proof">
      Our rules ship as <a href="/vetrina/ai-kit/">ai-kit</a>, an open-source CLI that detects a project's languages and
      installs the conventions, skills and commands that match.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="consulting-offer">
    <span class="consulting-offer-label">03 / Build</span>
    <h3 class="consulting-offer-title">Build the tool the agent needs</h3>
    <p>
      A CLI, an internal agent, or a tool server that fits your stack. The work is usually small and specific: enough
      tooling that the agent stops improvising around the gaps in your system.
    </p>
    <p class="consulting-offer-proof">
      We built
      <a href="/writing/how-coding-agents-work/building-pinocchio/">Pinocchio, a coding agent in five versions</a>, and
      two Go tools, <a href="/vetrina/fakedata/">fakedata</a> and <a href="/vetrina/db-diff/">db-diff</a>, that ship
      from a release pipeline we also wrote.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>

